rea's head was fired; government ministries and the civil service union called boycotts. Jang Dong-hyeok, the PPP chairman, accused Lee of \"collective bullying\" and \"another form of violence,\" framing the government's response as state overreach that turns a consumer issue into ideological warfare. The Gwangju uprising is the DPK's founding narrative — rallying around its defence fires the progressive base. The PPP's counter-framing may do the same for conservatives in exactly the races that are now competitive.\n\nOn the security side, Hanwha Aerospace unveiled the KAAV-II prototype — a next-generation amphibious assault vehicle with water speed above 20km/h against the legacy model's 13.2km/h, a 40mm autocannon capable of 85-degree elevation for anti-drone engagement, and an initial production timeline of 2029 against approved funding of roughly $1.78 billion. The Defense Ministry's first unification white paper under Lee formalized the \"peaceful coexistence\" doctrine: three explicit principles — respect the North Korean regime, rule out absorption unification, cease hostile acts — with sharply reduced references to both unification and North Korean human rights compared with prior editions. The analysis organization 38 North reads North Korea's concurrent constitutional revision in the opposite direction: Pyongyang is laying the groundwork for hostile two-state coexistence, not the peaceful kind, with new constitutional provisions for the defense industry and Kim Jong Un convening a military-wide commanders' meeting to reinforce front-line units along the southern border. Seoul is offering accommodation; Pyongyang is not interested in the same arrangement.",
